###["### Possible Next Three Steps\n\n1. Generate a new feature for driver age category by binning Age_of_Driver.\n\npython\n# Age_Category_of_Driver\n# Categorize the driver's age into meaningful bins (e.g., young, middle-aged, older drivers), based on Age_of_Driver. \n# Usefulness: This feature could capture relationships between age groups and driving patterns (e.g., younger drivers and risk-taking vs. older drivers and cautious driving), which may correlate with the sex of the driver.\nbins = [0, 20, 35, 50, 70, 100]\nlabels = ['Teen/Young Adult', 'Young Adult', 'Middle-Aged', 'Older Adult', 'Senior']\ndf['Age_Category_of_Driver'] = pd.cut(df['Age_of_Driver'], bins=bins, labels=labels)\nend\n\n2. Create interaction-based features by combining Vehicle_Type and Vehicle_Manoeuvre.\n\npython\n# Vehicle_Action_Combo\n# Combine Vehicle_Type and Vehicle_Manoeuvre to capture specific preference patterns for certain vehicle types and driving maneuvers.\n# Usefulness: This interaction feature could reveal behavioral patterns (e.g., sports cars and high-speed maneuvers could be associated with a particular gender, while sedans and cautious turns could indicate another).\ndf['Vehicle_Action_Combo'] = df['Vehicle_Type'].astype(str) + _ + df['Vehicle_Manoeuvre'].astype(str)\nend\n\n3. Compute a spatial urban/rural density ratio by combining Urban_or_Rural_Area, Number_of_Vehicles, and Number_of_Casualties.\n\npython\n# Urban_Rural_Density_Ratio\n# Ratio of the combined number of vehicles and casualties in urban vs rural areas.\n# Usefulness: Urban and rural settings may have distinct gender-based driving patterns, and areas with higher traffic density could indicate different sex distributions for drivers.\ndf['Urban_Rural_Density_Ratio'] = (df['Number_of_Vehicles'] + df['Number_of_Casualties']) / df['Urban_or_Rural_Area']\nend\n\n### Dropping Unnecessary Columns\nCertain columns may add noise or redundancy to the model and can be dropped based on lack of predictive power or 