y of samples (Median = 0, Q3 = 0), indicating limited relevance for predicting driver sex.\ndf.drop(columns=['2nd_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Casualty_Reference is dropped\n# Casualty_Reference is merely an identifier and does not contain inherent predictive information for the sex of the driver.\ndf.drop(columns=['Casualty_Reference'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nThese steps simplify feature engineering, focus on intuitive real-world variable interactions, and remove noisy or irrelevant columns to improve downstream model performance.", "python\n# Driver_Age_Normalized_by_Vehicle_Age\n# Feature description: Normalize Age_of_Driver by Age_of_Vehicle using z-score. This captures the relative age of the driver compared to the vehicle they drive.\n# Usefulness: Drivers of certain ages may show preferences for vehicle age, potentially correlating with gender differences in vehicle selection behavior.\ndf['Driver_Age_Normalized_by_Vehicle_Age'] = (df['Age_of_Driver'] - df['Age_of_Vehicle'].mean()) / df['Age_of_Vehicle'].std()\nend\n\npython\n# Vehicle_Impact_Severity\n# Feature description: Combine Number_of_Vehicles and Number_of_Casualties as an aggregate measure of accident severity.\n# Usefulness: Accident severity may indirectly indicate driver behavior, which could exhibit correlations with gender-specific driving patterns.\ndf['Vehicle_Impact_Severity'] = df['Number_of_Vehicles'] + df['Number_of_Casualties']\nend\n\npython\n# Urban_Rural_Area_by_Location_Density\n# Feature description: Create an interaction feature by dividing Urban_or_Rural_Area by the density derived from Location_Easting_OSGR and Location_Northing_OSGR.\n# Usefulness: Spatial dynamics may reveal insights into how gender responds to driving environments like urban or rural areas.\ndf['Urban_Rural_Area_by_Location_Density'] = (df['Urban_or_Rural_Area'] / (df['Location_Easting_OSGR'] + df['Location_Northing_OSGR']))\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Casualty_Reference is dropped\n# Casualty_Reference is purely an identifier and does not provide any predictive value for the sex of the driver.\ndf.drop(columns=['Casualty_Reference'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Longitude is dropped\n# Longitude is highly granular and spatial patterns are already captured through other broader features such as Urban_or_Rural_Area or Location_Easting_OSGR.\ndf.drop(columns=['Longitude'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 2nd_Road_Number is dropped\n# The column mostly contains zeros or missing values, and its limited variability makes it irrelevant for prediction.\ndf.drop(columns=['2nd_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Possible Next Three Steps\n\n1. Driver-to-Casualty Age Ratio\n\npython\n# Driver_to_Casualty_Age_Ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of Age_of_Driver to Age_of_Casualty.\n# Usefulness: This captures the relationship between the driver's age and the casualty's age, which could reveal preferences or tendencies (e.g., younger drivers traveling with older passengers or vice versa), possibly linked to gender differences.\ndf['Driver_to_Casualty_Age_Ratio'] = df['Age_of_Driver'] / (df['Age_of_Casualty'] + 1)  # Add 1 to avoid division by zero\nend\n\n2. Normalized Engine Capacity\n\npython\n# Normalized_Engine_Capacity\n# Feature description: Engine capacity normalized by the z-score method.\n# Usefulness: This standardizes engine capacity, capturing relative differences, and could highlight gender-specific preferences for vehicle engine power.\ndf['Normalized_Engine_Capacity'] = (df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'] - df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'].mean()) / df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'].std()\nend\n\n3. Urban/Rural Vehicle Impact Severity\n\npython\n# Urban_Rural_Vehicle_Impact_Severity\n# Feature description: Multiply Urban_or_Rural_Area by Vehicle_Impact_Severity.\n# Usefulness: This combines accident severity with the urban/rural setting, which might indicate gender-specific behavior in different environments during high-severity incidents.\ndf['Urban_Rural_Vehicle_Impact_Severity'] = df['Urban_or_Rural_Area'] * df['Vehicle_Impact_Severity']\nend\n\n### Dropping Unnecessary Columns\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Longitude is dropped\n# Longitude is highly granular with minimal direct relevance to predicting the driver's gender. Broader spatial features like Urban_or_Rural_Area already capture location information meaningfully.\ndf.drop(columns=['Longitude'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 2nd_Road_Number is dropped\n# 2nd_Road_Number contains mostly zeros or missing values, providing little to no meaningful variance for the prediction task.\ndf.drop(columns=['2nd_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Casualty_Reference is dropped\n# Casualty_Reference is an identifier with no intrinsic predictive value for the driver's gender.\ndf.drop(columns=['Casualty_Reference'], inplace=True)\nend"), with improvements -
